              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Origin of the City</strong><br>\nJerusalem is a very ancient city with a history dating back thousands of years. It is located in the Middle East and is known for its religious significance for three of the world's largest faiths: Judaism, Christianity, and Islam. Archaeological findings suggest that the area was already inhabited in the 4th millennium BC. Throughout the centuries, the city has been the stage for many historical events.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Significance for Judaism</strong><br>\nFor Jews, Jerusalem is the spiritual heart. The city hosts the Western Wall, which is a remnant of the Second Temple destroyed by the Romans in 70 AD. The Temple Mount is also the place where, according to tradition, Abraham was to sacrifice his son Isaac. Many Jewish festivals and rituals are closely associated with the city, and it is also an important location for Jewish pilgrims.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Significance for Christianity</strong><br>\nJerusalem is also a holy city for Christians. According to Christian tradition, this is the place where Jesus Christ was crucified and resurrected. The Via Dolorosa, Christ's path of suffering, and the Church of the Holy Sepulchre are important pilgrimage sites. Every year, Christians from around the world travel to Jerusalem to visit these and other holy sites.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
